---
/**
 * Introduction:  what `@spell/ui` is, the philosophy (from `docs/plan.md` "Context"), where to go next.
 */
import Example from "../components/Example.astro"
import Section from "../components/Section.astro"
import Docs from "../layouts/Docs.astro"
import { url } from "../lib/nav"
---

<Docs
  title="@spell/ui"
  summary="Fomantic UI reborn as ui-* custom elements on a modern CSS foundation, usable from any framework or plain HTML."
>
  <p>
    Fomantic UI has the best vocabulary of any widget set:  <code>ui small primary basic icon button</code> reads like a
    sentence.  <code>@spell/ui</code> keeps that vocabulary and rebuilds everything under it:  shadow DOM, cascade
    layers, OKLCH tokens, container and style queries, anchor positioning, <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code> and popover,
    with accessibility built in.
  </p>

  <Section title="Philosophy">
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Fomantic's vocabulary, kept.</strong>  Noun-first class grammar, and the Types / Content / States /
        Variations taxonomy on every page.  If you know Fomantic, you know this.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Modern CSS, not LESS + jQuery.</strong>  Nesting, <code>@layer</code>, anchor positioning,
        <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code> / popover, container queries, <code>:has()</code>, form-associated custom
        elements and <code>ElementInternals</code> ARIA.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Any framework, or none.</strong>  One package of <code>ui-*</code> custom elements:  rich data as JS
        properties, primitives as attributes, composed <code>ui-*</code> events.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Accessible by default.</strong>  Semantic shadow markup (<code>&lt;button&gt;</code>, never a
        <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> where an element exists), APG keyboard patterns, focus management, reduced motion.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Complete, and tested.</strong>  The functional side Semantic UI React never covered (Calendar, Slider,
        Toast, Flyout, form validation, API), each with unit, integration, accessibility and visual tests.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Section>

  <Section title="At a glance" description="Every example on this site is live:  what you see is the code below it.">
    <Example
      title="The grammar"
      description="Attributes on the element become Fomantic's class string inside its shadow root."
      class="ui-stack ui-gap-s"
    >
      <code>&lt;ui-button size="small" color="primary" basic&gt;</code>
      <span class="ui-muted">renders, inside its shadow root:</span>
      <code>&lt;button class="ui small primary basic button"&gt;</code>
    </Example>
    <Example
      title="Utilities"
      description="Layout and text utilities work on your own markup, and inside components."
      class="ui-cluster"
    >
      <span class="site-box">ui-cluster</span>
      <span class="site-box ui-bold">ui-bold</span>
      <span class="site-box ui-italic">ui-italic</span>
      <span class="site-box ui-text-uppercase">ui-text-uppercase</span>
    </Example>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Where next">
    <ul>
      <li><a href={url("/getting-started/")}>Getting started</a>:  install, and the framework contract.</li>
      <li><a href={url("/grammar/")}>Grammar</a>:  how attributes become classes.</li>
      <li><a href={url("/theming/")}>Theming</a>:  tokens, layers, the app stylesheet, themes.</li>
      <li><a href={url("/utilities/")}>Utilities</a>:  <code>ui-stack</code>, <code>ui-cluster</code> and friends.</li>
      <li><a href={url("/icons/")}>Icons</a>:  every Font Awesome 7 Free icon, by name.</li>
      <li><a href={url("/components/")}>Components</a>:  the elements themselves, as they land.</li>
    </ul>
  </Section>
</Docs>
